{
 "id": "2026-11-21-m1",
 "title": "Your Wi-Fi and your microwave use the SAME wave 📶",
 "description": "Wi-Fi and microwave ovens both use the 2.4 GHz band. So why doesn't your router cook you? Power: an oven pumps roughly 700-1,100 watts into a sealed metal box, while a Wi-Fi router is capped at about 0.1 watt (100 mW), spread through your house, about 10,000 times weaker. Inside the oven the field flips direction billions of times a second; water molecules are polar and keep trying to flip with it, and that jostling turns into heat. 2.45 GHz is not water's 'resonant frequency' (a popular myth): water absorbs most around 10-20 GHz, and 2.45 GHz was chosen because it reaches deeper into food (and sits in a licence-free band). The wave is ~12 cm long, the holes in the door's metal screen ~1 mm, so it can't get out... mostly: a tiny leak can still disturb 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi nearby.\n\nSources: Make Tech Easier (2.4 GHz and water); Illinois Physics Van (door screen); SlashGear (Wi-Fi interference); Physics Forums summary (oven vs router power).",
